WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.207
[MUSIC]

00:00:08.207 --> 00:00:13.239
There is a national dilemma on this,
and a real debate of whether

00:00:13.239 --> 00:00:18.657
in particular detest sexual assault
kits from acquaintance rapes.

00:00:18.657 --> 00:00:23.001
And we did a cost benefit
analysis of this, found for

00:00:23.001 --> 00:00:27.742
every $1 you spend testing
a sexual assault kit you save

00:00:27.742 --> 00:00:32.306
$81 on average,
to avert future sexual assaults.

00:00:32.306 --> 00:00:38.271
There's always been a moral argument
to test this large backlog of sexual

00:00:38.271 --> 00:00:44.824
assault kits and now we buttress that
argument with a strong economic argument.

00:00:44.824 --> 00:00:50.754
[MUSIC]

00:00:50.754 --> 00:00:54.199
Unfortunately what has
happened often in the past,

00:00:54.199 --> 00:00:58.201
is that the hospital sends
the kit to the police department.

00:00:58.201 --> 00:01:03.310
And there are currently around
400,000 sexual assault kits

00:01:03.310 --> 00:01:09.054
just sitting in warehouses in police
departments throughout the country.

00:01:09.054 --> 00:01:14.055
So think about that 400,000
sexual assault victims

00:01:14.055 --> 00:01:20.767
go through this incredibly unpleasant
exam after a traumatic sexual assault.

00:01:20.767 --> 00:01:26.009
They do it so that they hope they
will find the offender by doing this.

00:01:26.009 --> 00:01:30.343
And then they kit just is sitting around,
and they never investigate it at all.

00:01:30.343 --> 00:01:36.082
[MUSIC]

00:01:36.082 --> 00:01:40.861
When a victim of a sexual assault
goes to the police department,

00:01:40.861 --> 00:01:46.715
the police encourage them to go to the
hospital to get a medical forensic exam.

00:01:46.715 --> 00:01:51.805
And there the nurses try to get some
foreign DNA from the victim, and they take

00:01:51.805 --> 00:01:56.912
these swabs and put them in a box, and
that's called the sexual assault kit.

00:01:56.912 --> 00:01:59.886
And then the hospital sends it
back to the police department.

00:01:59.886 --> 00:02:03.552
And ideally what happens at
that point is that the police

00:02:03.552 --> 00:02:08.244
department sends it to a lab,
the lab tries to get DNA from these swabs.

00:02:08.244 --> 00:02:14.408
If they get DNA, they run it against
the national database of felons.

00:02:14.408 --> 00:02:18.993
And they try to get a hit, and if they
get a hit against that database, so

00:02:18.993 --> 00:02:23.961
the DNA in the swab is the same as the DNA
in the database, then ideally this hit

00:02:23.961 --> 00:02:28.259
would become a conviction,
you'd actually put someone in jail.

00:02:28.259 --> 00:02:34.183
[MUSIC]

00:02:34.183 --> 00:02:39.583
The cost of testing a sexual
assault kit is around $1,600.

00:02:39.583 --> 00:02:46.526
And the average amount that we save
by testing it is around $130,000,

00:02:46.526 --> 00:02:50.852
which is 81 times bigger than the 1,600.

00:02:50.852 --> 00:02:54.576
So, again,
every $1 we spend we're saving about $81.

00:02:54.576 --> 00:02:58.041
That's an incredibly conservative number,

00:02:58.041 --> 00:03:02.941
the actual number instead of 81
is probably a couple thousand.

00:03:02.941 --> 00:03:07.909
Because the chances of conviction now
are much higher than they were in

00:03:07.909 --> 00:03:10.855
the data that we estimated from New York,

00:03:10.855 --> 00:03:15.928
because the statute of limitations
are much larger now in most states.

00:03:15.928 --> 00:03:21.397
[MUSIC]

00:03:21.397 --> 00:03:27.548
There's many other benefits from testing
these kits that we didn't quantify.

00:03:27.548 --> 00:03:33.042
So one big one is we didn't quantify
the costs associated with nonsexual crime.

00:03:33.042 --> 00:03:38.148
Which are actually larger than the costs
associated with future sexual assaults

00:03:38.148 --> 00:03:43.417
because these people on average commit
more nonsexual crimes than sexual crimes.

00:03:43.417 --> 00:03:47.052
It also brings closure to the victims.

00:03:47.052 --> 00:03:53.606
It also, an interesting study people
find that it helps deter future crimes.

00:03:53.606 --> 00:03:58.770
If you actually get the kit and
you put this person into the database,

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:02.865
that once this person finds
out there in the database,

00:04:02.865 --> 00:04:05.820
they will commit fewer future crimes.

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:11.428
There are some people out there that
are just serial sexual offenders,

00:04:11.428 --> 00:04:17.145
and not just sexual offenders but
many other violent nonsexual felons.

00:04:17.145 --> 00:04:21.653
And we have the technology,
we have the wherewithal,

00:04:21.653 --> 00:04:26.954
we know how to get them off the street,
we just haven't done it.

00:04:26.954 --> 00:04:30.747
And there's an easy way to get some
of these people off the street, and

00:04:30.747 --> 00:04:31.659
we should do it.

00:04:31.659 --> 00:04:39.653
[MUSIC]

